  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on GPS trajectory data from a single outdoor recreation platform, which may reflect the specific demographics and behaviors of its user base rather than all tourists.",
      "what": "This study developed a journey-based semantic embedding framework to identify nature-based tourism destinations using GPS trajectory data from an outdoor recreation platform on the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau. The framework maps destinations based on collective travel flows rather than static point-density, revealing that 63.2% of identified destinations overlapping national nature reserves lack formal management systems. Temporal analysis from 2017 onward shows a decline in newly emerging destinations and faster decline rates for newer sites compared to established ones.",
      "why": "It shifts the paradigm of tourism destination mapping from static spatial clusters to flow-based journeys, exposing linear destinations like highways and showing that administrative closures of nature reserves often redirect visitor flows to adjacent, unregulated areas rather than stopping them."
    }
